## 1. INTRODUCTION TO LIT

### 1.1 What is Lit?

**Lit** is a simple, fast, and lightweight library for building **Web Components**. Created by the Google Chrome team (formerly Polymer Project), Lit provides a thin declarative layer on top of the native Web Components standards.

- **Created by**: Google (Polymer team)
- **First Release**: Lit 2.0 (2021) — successor to LitElement 2.x and lit-html 1.x
- **Current Version**: Lit 3.x
- **Size**: ~5 KB (minified + gzipped)
- **License**: BSD-3-Clause
- **GitHub**: https://github.com/lit/lit
- **Docs**: https://lit.dev

### 1.2 Why Lit?

| Feature | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| **Web Standards** | Built on native Web Components (Custom Elements, Shadow DOM, Templates) |
| **Tiny Size** | ~5 KB gzipped — no virtual DOM, no framework overhead |
| **Fast Rendering** | lit-html uses tagged template literals for efficient DOM updates |
| **Interoperable** | Works with any framework — React, Angular, Vue, or vanilla JS |
| **Simple API** | Minimal learning curve — just classes, decorators, and template literals |
| **No Build Required** | Can work with native ES modules in modern browsers |
| **TypeScript First** | First-class TypeScript support with decorators |
| **SSR Support** | Server-side rendering via `@lit-labs/ssr` |

### 1.3 Web Components Standards

Lit builds on these native browser APIs:

```
Web Components Standards
├── Custom Elements API    → Define new HTML elements (customElements.define)
├── Shadow DOM             → Encapsulated DOM tree and scoped CSS
├── HTML Templates         → <template> and <slot> elements
└── ES Modules             → Standard JavaScript module system
```

### 1.4 Lit Package Structure

```
lit (main package — recommended)
├── lit-html          → Template rendering engine
├── lit-element       → Base class for web components
├── @lit/reactive-element → Base reactive class (no rendering)
└── @lit-labs/*       → Experimental features
    ├── @lit-labs/ssr          → Server-side rendering
    ├── @lit-labs/context      → Context protocol (now @lit/context)
    ├── @lit-labs/task         → Async task controller
    ├── @lit-labs/router       → Client-side routing
    ├── @lit-labs/motion       → Animations
    └── @lit-labs/observers    → Resize/Intersection observers
```

### 1.5 Browser Support

Lit 3.x supports all modern evergreen browsers:
- Chrome, Edge, Firefox, Safari (latest 2 versions)
- No IE11 support (Lit 2.x had polyfills for IE11)

